type: framework
namespace: SEBI
version: "2026-08"
anchor_version: ">=6.0.0"
maintainer: "Anchor Core"
opt_in: true
source: >
  SEBI (Intermediaries) (Amendment) Regulations, 2025 [Regulation 16C];
  parallel SECC and DP Regulations amendments; SEBI AI/ML reporting
  circulars (Jan-May 2019); SEBI Consultation Paper on AI/ML Guidelines
  (June 2025).
source_url: "https://www.sebi.gov.in/sebi_data/meetingfiles/dec-2024/1735042007618_1.pdf"
source_date: "2019-2026"
credit: >
  Securities and Exchange Board of India. Three distinct instruments,
  three distinct legal weights — see verification_note.
verification_note: >
  MAJOR CORRECTION from prior version: the previous file cited ONLY the
  June 2025 consultation paper (not yet binding, comments closed July
  11, 2025) as its source. It omitted two ALREADY-BINDING instruments
  entirely:
    (1) Regulation 16C, inserted by the SEBI (Intermediaries) (Amendment)
        Regulations, 2025, notified February 10, 2025, in effect
        immediately. Verified against the primary Board memorandum
        (SEBI meeting files, Dec 2024) — not a secondary summary.
    (2) Three 2019 AI/ML reporting circulars (brokers/DPs — Jan 4, 2019;
        MIIs — Jan 31, 2019; mutual funds/AMCs — May 9, 2019), still in
        force and actively enforced — harmonised into a cross-exchange
        filing mechanism as recently as October 2025 (MSE Circular
        MSE/MEM/17921/2025).
  Both are added below as SEBI-REG16C and SEBI-CIR2019. These are
  legally stronger than SEBI-001 through SEBI-010, which derive from
  the still-non-binding June 2025 consultation paper — despite the
  lower rule IDs suggesting otherwise at a glance. Read legal_status
  below before citing any SEBI-* rule's obligation strength to a client
  or auditor.
legal_status: >
  SEBI-REG16C and SEBI-CIR2019 are BINDING LAW, in force now.
  Non-compliance with Reg 16C triggers SEBI Act 1992 enforcement
  (monetary sanctions, cease-and-desist, suspension). SEBI-001 through
  SEBI-010 derive from the June 2025 consultation paper, which is
  PROPOSED GUIDANCE ONLY as of this version's date — comments closed
  July 11, 2025, not yet finalized into binding regulation. Do not
  describe SEBI-001 through SEBI-010 as "SEBI-mandated" in binding-law
  terms in client-facing material; SEBI-REG16C and SEBI-CIR2019 are the
  rules that can accurately be described that way today.
content_verification_note: >
  SEBI-001 through SEBI-010 below are retained from the prior version
  of this file, substantively unchanged except for legal_status framing
  and added primitives. Their content was cross-checked against
  multiple secondary legal-commentary summaries of the June 2025
  paper (consistent across sources) but NOT against the consultation
  paper's own verbatim text, which was not independently fetched for
  this revision. Treat as reliable but not yet primary-source-verified
  to the same standard as RBI-* or SEBI-REG16C/CIR2019 below.
id_gap_note: >
  SEBI-009 does not exist in either this or the prior version of this
  file (the sequence goes 008 to 010) — not corrected here since there
  was no way to verify what, if anything, was intended for that slot
  without the consultation paper's verbatim numbered list. Worth
  checking against the actual paper if/when it's obtained.
layer_2_status: >
  Rules marked obligation_type: provenance or audit depend on
  AnchorRuntime (Layer 2) and the Decision Audit Chain (DAC).
  Layer 2 is currently in development.
seal: "sha256:PENDING"

rules:

  - id: "SEBI-REG16C"
    name: "Sole AI/ML Liability — Regulation 16C (BINDING)"
    original_id: "Regulation 16C, SEBI (Intermediaries) (Amendment) Regulations, 2025; parallel amendments to SECC and DP Regulations"
    maps_to: "DAC-AuditEntry"
    obligation_type: provenance
    primitives:
      action: assign_liability
      object: ai_ml_tool_usage_consequences
      context: in_house_or_third_party_procured
      authority: sebi_act_1992_enforcement
      flow: ai_output_and_data_handling_to_sole_responsibility
    anchor_mechanism: >
      chain_hash + signature in AuditEntry provides the non-repudiation
      proof establishing which regulated entity's key signed off on a
      given AI decision — directly relevant since Reg 16C makes
      liability non-transferable to vendors regardless of who built the
      tool. Layer 2 in development.
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "blocker"
    description: >
      Verified against the primary SEBI Board memorandum (Dec 2024
      meeting files): any person regulated by SEBI using AI/ML tools —
      whether designed in-house or procured from a third-party
      technology provider, irrespective of scale of adoption — is
      SOLELY responsible for: (1) the privacy, security, and integrity
      of investor/stakeholder data, including data held in a fiduciary
      capacity, throughout all processes involved; (2) the output
      arising from usage of the AI/ML tool or technique; and (3)
      compliance with all applicable laws in force. Notified February
      10, 2025, in effect immediately — this is currently the single
      most legally significant AI-related obligation any SEBI-regulated
      entity faces, and it was entirely absent from the prior version
      of this file.

  - id: "SEBI-CIR2019"
    name: "Quarterly AI/ML Inventory Reporting (BINDING, ongoing)"
    original_id: >
      SEBI/HO/MIRSD/DOS2/CIR/P/2019/10 (Jan 4, 2019, brokers/DPs);
      SEBI/HO/MRD/DOP1/CIR/P/2019/24 (Jan 31, 2019, MIIs);
      SEBI/HO/IMD/DF5/CIR/P/2019/63 (May 9, 2019, mutual funds/AMCs);
      harmonised via NSE cross-exchange filing (Jul 2025) and MSE
      Circular MSE/MEM/17921/2025 (Oct 7, 2025)
    maps_to: "DAC-AuditEntry"
    obligation_type: disclosure
    primitives:
      action: report
      object: ai_ml_application_inventory
      context: quarterly_recurring_filing
      authority: sebi_regulator
      flow: internal_ai_inventory_to_regulator_filing
    anchor_mechanism: >
      model_id + model_version per AuditEntry constitutes the technical
      inventory record. anchor audit --report could be extended to
      export directly in whatever format the harmonised NSE/MSE
      cross-exchange filing expects — not yet built as a dedicated
      export target, flagged as a real scope item given this is a
      recurring, currently-enforced filing obligation, not a
      theoretical one.
    severity: "error"
    min_severity: "error"
    description: >
      Verified against the primary Board memorandum: since 2019, stock
      brokers/DPs, market infrastructure institutions (exchanges,
      clearing corporations, depositories), and mutual funds/AMCs/
      trustees must report AI/ML applications and systems to SEBI on a
      quarterly basis (within 15 days of quarter-end), including NIL
      submissions when no AI/ML tool is in use. Confirmed real-world use
      categories from the Board memo: brokers/DPs use AI/ML for
      surveillance, order execution, and KYC/AML; MIIs for surveillance,
      risk management, generative AI document/order review, cybersecurity,
      and chatbots; mutual funds for surveillance, chatbot-based customer
      service, cybersecurity, and customer segmentation. This is a
      live, recurring compliance obligation happening today — the
      October 2025 MSE circular confirms it was still being actively
      harmonised and enforced as of last year, not a dormant rule.

  - id: "SEBI-001"
    name: "Senior Management Oversight — Designated Responsibility"
    original_id: "June 2025 Consultation Paper — Governance principle (not yet binding)"
    maps_to: "ETH-003"
    obligation_type: disclosure
    primitives:
      action: designate
      object: senior_management_ai_oversight_role
      context: proposed_not_yet_binding
      authority: regulated_entity_board
      flow: policy_to_governance_artifact
    anchor_mechanism: >
      Board-approved policy.anchor with sealed constitution.anchor
      constitutes the governance policy artifact. anchor audit
      --report generates the oversight documentation for senior
      management sign-off.
    severity: "error"
    min_severity: "warning"
    description: >
      PROPOSED (not yet binding): market participants using AI/ML
      would be required to designate senior management with technical
      expertise to oversee AI tool performance and control, with
      board-level oversight and named accountability. Severity lowered
      from prior version's "blocker" to "error" to reflect this rule's
      actual current legal status — see legal_status field above.

  - id: "SEBI-002"
    name: "Model Validation, Documentation and Interpretability"
    original_id: "June 2025 Consultation Paper — Model governance principle (not yet binding)"
    maps_to: "ETH-002"
    obligation_type: detection
    primitives:
      action: document_validate
      object: ai_model_logic_and_outcomes
      context: proposed_not_yet_binding
      authority: regulated_entity
      flow: model_behavior_to_explainable_documentation
    anchor_mechanism: >
      ETH-002 explainability absence fires on black-box model usage.
      TRANS-* violation category covers transparency and documentation
      gaps. PROV-001 fires on AI output without model version.
      Layer 1 detection active now.
    severity: "warning"
    min_severity: "warning"
    description: >
      PROPOSED (not yet binding): market participants would be
      required to maintain validation documentation and ensure AI
      model interpretability, with outcomes explainable, traceable,
      and repeatable. Severity lowered from prior version to reflect
      non-binding status.

  - id: "SEBI-003"
    name: "Periodic Accuracy Reporting to SEBI"
    original_id: "June 2025 Consultation Paper — Reporting principle (not yet binding)"
    maps_to: "DAC-AuditLog"
    obligation_type: audit
    primitives:
      action: report
      object: ai_ml_accuracy_and_audit_findings
      context: proposed_not_yet_binding
      authority: sebi_regulator
      flow: internal_audit_to_periodic_regulator_disclosure
    anchor_mechanism: >
      anchor audit --report generates JSON and Markdown accuracy
      and compliance reports. /audit endpoint exposes the full
      audit chain for regulator access. Layer 2 in development
      for full continuous reporting support.
    severity: "warning"
    min_severity: "warning"
    description: >
      PROPOSED (not yet binding): market participants would be
      required to share AI/ML accuracy results and audit findings with
      SEBI periodically, in structured form. Distinct from SEBI-CIR2019
      above, which is the already-binding quarterly inventory filing —
      this proposed rule would add accuracy/performance reporting on
      top of the existing inventory disclosure.

  - id: "SEBI-004"
    name: "5-Year Input and Output Data Retention"
    original_id: "June 2025 Consultation Paper — Data retention principle (not yet binding)"
    maps_to: "DAC-AuditEntry"
    obligation_type: provenance
    primitives:
      action: retain
      object: ai_input_and_output_data
      context: five_year_statutory_limitation_alignment
      authority: regulated_entity
      flow: decision_data_to_long_term_retention
    anchor_mechanism: >
      DAC AuditEntry records input_hash + output_hash per decision
      with ISO 8601 timestamp. Append-only log provides 5-year
      retention with tamper-evident integrity. Layer 2 in development.
    severity: "warning"
    min_severity: "warning"
    description: >
      PROPOSED (not yet binding): market participants would be
      required to maintain model documentation and retain input/output
      data for at least 5 years, aligning with the statutory limitation
      period for most SEBI enforcement actions. Severity lowered from
      prior version's "blocker" to reflect non-binding status.

  - id: "SEBI-005"
    name: "Segregated Testing Environment Before Deployment"
    original_id: "June 2025 Consultation Paper — Testing principle (not yet binding)"
    maps_to: "LEG-002"
    obligation_type: detection
    primitives:
      action: test_isolate
      object: ai_model_pre_deployment
      context: stressed_and_unstressed_market_conditions
      authority: regulated_entity
      flow: shadow_testing_to_deployment_gate
    anchor_mechanism: >
      anchor check in CI/CD acts as the pre-deployment governance
      gate. Diamond Cage WASM sandbox provides the segregated
      runtime testing environment for high-risk operations.
      Layer 1 active now.
    severity: "warning"
    min_severity: "warning"
    description: >
      PROPOSED (not yet binding): AI/ML models would need to be tested
      in a segregated environment before deployment, with shadow
      testing against live traffic and validation under both stressed
      and unstressed market conditions.

  - id: "SEBI-006"
    name: "Continuous Monitoring as AI Models Evolve"
    original_id: "June 2025 Consultation Paper — Monitoring principle (not yet binding)"
    maps_to: "SUP-003"
    obligation_type: provenance
    primitives:
      action: monitor
      object: ai_model_behavioral_drift
      context: post_deployment_continuous
      authority: regulated_entity
      flow: version_change_to_drift_detection
    anchor_mechanism: >
      SUP-003 versioning drift violation fires on undeclared model
      version changes. AnchorRuntime continuous eval provides
      post-deployment monitoring. model_version in AuditEntry
      per decision enables drift detection. Layer 2 in development.
    severity: "warning"
    min_severity: "warning"
    description: >
      PROPOSED (not yet binding): continuous monitoring systems would
      be required beyond one-time testing, since AI models may change
      behavior over time — model drift detection positioned as an
      ongoing regulatory expectation rather than a best practice.

  - id: "SEBI-007"
    name: "Investor Disclosures for AI-Driven Decisions"
    original_id: "June 2025 Consultation Paper — Disclosure principle (not yet binding)"
    maps_to: "ETH-002"
    obligation_type: disclosure
    primitives:
      action: disclose
      object: ai_product_features_risks_and_accuracy
      context: investor_comprehensible_language
      authority: consumer_investor
      flow: model_characteristics_to_investor_notice
    anchor_mechanism: >
      ETH-002 explainability absence detection. adverse_action_reasons()
      provides investor-facing reason codes. anchor audit --report
      generates disclosure artifacts. Layer 1 active now.
    severity: "warning"
    min_severity: "warning"
    description: >
      PROPOSED (not yet binding): market participants using AI in
      customer-facing decisions would need to disclose product
      features, purpose, risks, model accuracy, fees, and data quality
      in investor-comprehensible (not just technical) language, plus
      establish AI-specific grievance redressal mechanisms.

  - id: "SEBI-008"
    name: "No Discriminatory AI Outputs — Fairness"
    original_id: "June 2025 Consultation Paper — Fairness principle (not yet binding)"
    maps_to: "ETH-001"
    obligation_type: detection
    primitives:
      action: detect_bias
      object: ai_model_output_disparity
      context: client_customer_group_fairness
      authority: internal_data_science_function
      flow: dataset_bias_to_fairness_control
    anchor_mechanism: >
      ETH-001 bias and discrimination detection active in Layer 1.
      BIAS-* violation category fires on protected attribute usage
      in feature vectors and decision outputs.
    severity: "warning"
    min_severity: "warning"
    description: >
      PROPOSED (not yet binding): AI/ML models would need processes
      to identify and remove dataset bias, with mandatory bias-awareness
      training for data scientists developing AI systems for
      SEBI-regulated market participants.

  - id: "SEBI-010"
    name: "Third-Party AI Vendor Accountability — No Transfer"
    original_id: "June 2025 Consultation Paper principle — NOTE: this specific liability position is now ALSO binding law via SEBI-REG16C above, independent of the consultation paper's eventual finalization"
    maps_to: "DAC-AuditEntry"
    obligation_type: provenance
    primitives:
      action: retain_liability
      object: third_party_ai_vendor_output
      context: contractual_sla_notwithstanding
      authority: regulated_entity
      flow: vendor_model_output_to_non_transferable_liability
    anchor_mechanism: >
      Non-repudiation chain — the deploying firm's AuditEntry
      signed with their key proves ownership of every AI decision
      regardless of which vendor's model produced it. Layer 2
      in development.
    severity: "blocker"
    min_severity: "blocker"
    description: >
      Severity kept at "blocker" (unlike SEBI-001 through 008) because
      this specific position — vendor liability does not transfer to
      SEBI-regulated entities — is independently and currently binding
      via Regulation 16C (SEBI-REG16C above), regardless of whether the
      broader June 2025 consultation paper is ever finalized. SLAs with
      AI vendors may include regulatory-compliance obligations
      contractually, but that does not shift the regulatory liability
      SEBI places on the market participant itself.
